Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — this is a good set of concerns to prepare for. A few important points up front, then practical questions and testing you can bring to Dr. Wilson.

Urgent safety note
- You said your last blood pressure was 320. If that means a systolic reading of ~320 mmHg (or any extremely high reading), that is a potential emergency. Please re-check the reading right away (different cuff, both arms if possible). If it’s truly that high or you have symptoms (chest pain, severe headache, vision changes, confusion, shortness of breath, weakness), go to the nearest ED or call emergency services now. Don’t wait for the visit.

Osteopenia — what to consider and ask about
- Confirm diagnosis: ask whether you have had a DEXA (bone density) scan and when. Osteopenia = T-score between −1.0 and −2.5; osteoporosis ≤ −2.5.
- Risk calculation: request a FRAX 10-year fracture risk calculation (includes age, sex, weight/height, prior fractures, smoking, steroids, alcohol, secondary causes).
  - Typical US treatment thresholds: consider pharmacologic treatment if 10-year hip fracture risk ≥3% or major osteoporotic fracture risk ≥20% (different guidelines exist — discuss how your PCP applies them).
- Labs to check for secondary causes: serum calcium, 25‑OH vitamin D, PTH (if calcium abnormal), TSH (you’re on levothyroxine — overreplacement can worsen bone loss), basic metabolic panel (renal function), and possibly celiac serology, testosterone if applicable, and a 24‑hour urine calcium if indicated.
- Bone-preserving measures:
  - Vitamin D supplementation (often 800–1000 IU/day or adjusted to get 25‑OH D to target range).
  - Dietary calcium to ~1,000–1,200 mg/day (supplement if needed).
  - Weight‑bearing and resistance exercise, balance training to reduce fall risk.
  - Avoid smoking; limit alcohol.
  - Review medications that increase fall risk (you mentioned gabapentin — discuss fall risk/side effects).
- When to start meds:
  - If DEXA shows osteoporosis (T ≤ −2.5) → usually start therapy.
  - If osteopenia and FRAX exceeds thresholds above, or you’ve had a fragility fracture → treat.
  - First-line meds are usually bisphosphonates (oral alendronate or risedronate; IV zoledronic acid for some patients). Other options include denosumab or anabolic agents for high-risk patients. Ask about renal function, dental health (dental exam before certain meds), and potential side effects.
- Monitoring: repeat DEXA every 1–3 years depending on treatment and risk; periodic labs as ordered.

Cardiology referral — do you need one?
Given your age (55) and risk factors (type 2 diabetes, prior stroke, and very high BP reading), cardiology evaluation is reasonable in many scenarios. Points to discuss with Dr. Wilson:
- Indications that commonly trigger referral:
  - Persistently uncontrolled hypertension despite several antihypertensives.
  - Evidence of coronary disease, heart failure, arrhythmia, or abnormal EKG.
  - Need for advanced risk stratification (stress testing, coronary CT angiography) given diabetes + stroke history.
  - Complex medication management for secondary prevention.
- What a cardiology visit might include: echocardiogram, resting EKG, ambulatory BP monitoring, stress test or coronary imaging if ischemia suspected, medication optimization.
- Also ask about vascular neurology/stroke clinic follow-up because prior stroke may warrant specialized secondary prevention.

What to ask Dr. James Wilson at your annual physical
Osteopenia / bone health
- “Have I had a DEXA scan? If so, what are my T-scores and FRAX 10‑year fracture risks?”
- “Should we check vitamin D, calcium, PTH, TSH and kidney function before deciding on treatment?”
- “Given my age, diabetes, and medications, do you recommend starting bone-specific medication now? If so, which and why?”
- “Any dental or renal issues that would affect choice of therapy?”

Cardiac / blood pressure
- “My last BP reading was 320 — can we recheck and review recent home readings? Is this an emergency?”
- “Given my diabetes and prior stroke, should you refer me to cardiology (or vascular neurology) for further assessment?”
- “What is my target BP and what tests do you recommend now (EKG, echocardiogram, ambulatory BP monitoring, lipid panel, etc.)?”
- “Do my current meds need adjustment or additions for better BP and cardiovascular risk control?”

Pre-visit labs and tests you can request or expect
- A1c, fasting lipid panel
- Basic metabolic panel (including creatinine), electrolytes
- Urine microalbumin / creatinine ratio
- TSH (because of levothyroxine), 25‑OH vitamin D, calcium, PTH if indicated
- DEXA scan if not done in the past 1–2 years (or sooner if clinically indicated)
- Resting EKG and possibly echocardiogram if there’s concern
- FRAX calculation (can be done in clinic)

Medication interactions / special considerations
- Review levothyroxine dosing — overreplacement (low TSH) can increase bone loss. Ask for recent TSH result.
- Gabapentin can increase dizziness/fall risk — mention this when discussing fall prevention.
- Check renal function before bisphosphonates; dental evaluation recommended before IV bisphosphonates or denosumab.

