Below are practical, prioritized items to review and questions to take to your PCP (Dr. James Wilson), divided into osteopenia and cardiovascular/cardiology referral.

Osteopenia management — what to check and ask
- Basics to confirm:
  - Do I have a recent DXA (bone density) scan? If yes, what are my T-scores (spine, total hip, femoral neck) and date?
  - If no DXA in the last 1–2 years, ask for one now.
- Risk assessment:
  - Ask the doctor to calculate your FRAX 10-year fracture risk (with and without BMD) — treatment for osteopenia often depends on FRAX thresholds (commonly: start therapy if 10-year major osteoporotic fracture risk ≥20% or hip fracture risk ≥3%, though thresholds vary).
  - Review secondary causes of bone loss (thyroid overreplacement, steroids, alcohol, smoking, low BMI, malabsorption, anticonvulsants).
- Labs to order:
  - Serum calcium, creatinine, 25-hydroxy vitamin D, TSH (because you take levothyroxine), serum alkaline phosphatase, and possibly urine calcium or celiac screening if indicated.
- Lifestyle and supplements:
  - Adequate calcium (usually 1,000–1,200 mg/day total from diet + supplement if needed) and vitamin D (common dosing 800–2,000 IU/day depending on level) — ask what dose is right for you based on vitamin D result and kidney function.
  - Weight-bearing and resistance exercise, balance training to reduce falls, smoking cessation, limit alcohol.
- Pharmacologic therapy considerations:
  - Bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, zoledronic acid) are typical first-line if treatment is indicated; alternatives include denosumab or raloxifene depending on individual risks and kidney function.
  - Discuss renal function and dental health (rare risk of osteonecrosis of the jaw with antiresorptives).
  - If you’re already on thyroid hormone, confirm TSH is not suppressed — chronically low TSH can worsen bone loss.
- Follow-up:
  - How often to repeat DXA (commonly 1–2 years initially, then longer if stable), and when to re-check labs.
- Specific questions to ask Dr. Wilson about osteopenia:
  - “Do my DXA results (or FRAX) indicate I should start medication now?”
  - “Which bone-protecting medication do you recommend given my kidney function and other meds?”
  - “Should we check TSH/vitamin D/renal function before starting therapy?”
  - “What lifestyle changes and fall-prevention measures should I do?”
  - “How often should we repeat DXA and labs?”

Cardiology referral — who needs it and why given your profile
- Your risk profile: age 55, type 2 diabetes, sleep apnea, history of stroke — all increase cardiovascular risk. Those factors make you higher risk, but referral to a cardiologist is guided by specific problems or test findings, not by risk alone.
