Below are focused items and concrete questions you can take to your PCP (Dr. James Wilson) for the annual physical, grouped so you can paste them into a message or bring a printed checklist.

What to bring to the visit
- List of all medications (name, dose, how often), supplements, and who prescribes each.
- Recent home BP log (dates/times/readings) and device info.
- Last lab results (A1c, lipids, kidney function), DEXA scan date/results (if any), and records of the prior stroke (type, date, imaging, discharge meds).
- CPAP usage data (hours/night) if you use CPAP for sleep apnea.
- Any symptoms since last visit (chest pain, shortness of breath, new weakness, dizziness, falls, increased fatigue).

Immediate questions about the very high BP
- I had a BP reading of 320 — is that possible and how should I proceed right now? (If symptomatic, advise immediate ER.)
- Should I come in for an urgent BP check today, or go to urgent care/ER?
- If home readings are high, should I start/adjust BP meds now or schedule same‑day evaluation?

Cardiology referral — suggested questions and reasons to ask for one
- Given my age (55), type 2 diabetes, sleep apnea, and prior stroke, do you recommend a referral to Colorado Cardiology for further cardiovascular risk assessment?
- What specific findings would prompt an automatic cardiology referral (e.g., uncontrolled BP, abnormal ECG, heart murmur, reduced ejection fraction, arrhythmia, recurrent TIAs)?
- Can we get an ECG and, if indicated, an echocardiogram or ambulatory heart rhythm monitor before deciding?
- Should I have carotid imaging or vascular neurology follow‑up given my stroke history?
- Would you consider ambulatory/outpatient BP monitoring or home BP titration protocol?

Osteopenia management — concrete questions/tests
- Can we check a DEXA scan now (if not done in past 2 years) and calculate my FRAX fracture risk?
- What is my current T‑score and 10‑year fracture risk? Based on that, do you recommend:
  - Lifestyle measures only (exercise, fall prevention),
  - Calcium and vitamin D supplementation (what doses you recommend),
  - Pharmacologic therapy (bisphosphonates or alternatives) — and why?
- Could my levothyroxine dose be affecting my bone density? Please check TSH level and adjust if iatrogenic hyperthyroidism is a concern.
- Labs to check for secondary causes: 25‑OH vitamin D, calcium, creatinine/eGFR, TSH, possibly PTH if abnormal calcium.
- Fall‑risk assessment and referral to physical therapy or balance training if indicated.

Diabetes and overall CV risk (things to review)
- Latest A1c and targets; any need to change diabetes meds for cardiovascular benefit?
- Lipid panel and whether I should start/adjust statin or consider other lipid therapies.
- Kidney function and urine albumin (for cardiovascular and bone safety considerations).
- Antiplatelet/anticoagulation review given prior stroke (are we optimized on secondary prevention?).
- Sleep apnea — is CPAP working; do we need a Sleep Medicine or Cardiology assessment for arrhythmia/risk?

Tests you can request at the visit
- DEXA scan (if none in past 2 years)
- FRAX calculation
- TSH, 25‑OH vitamin D, calcium, CMP (kidney function), CBC
- HbA1c, lipid panel, urine albumin/creatinine ratio
- ECG (and echo or ambulatory monitor if any abnormal findings)
- Ambulatory BP monitor or 24‑hr BP readings if home readings vary widely

Suggested wording to request a cardiology referral (one‑line script)
- “Given my age (55), type 2 diabetes, sleep apnea, and prior stroke — and a recent very high blood pressure reading — I’d like you to refer me to Colorado Cardiology for a comprehensive cardiovascular risk evaluation (ECG/echo/possible ambulatory monitoring). Do you agree this is warranted now?”

Suggested wording for osteopenia discussion
- “Please order a DEXA and FRAX calculation and review whether I meet criteria for pharmacologic osteoporosis treatment. Also check TSH and vitamin D to rule out secondary contributors to bone loss.”

