Lifestyle and medication notes you can discuss
- Osteopenia: weight‑bearing/resistance exercise, fall prevention, smoking cessation, limit alcohol, dietary calcium (~1,000–1,200 mg/day total depending on age/diet), vitamin D supplementation if low (discuss dose after testing).  
- BP/Stroke risk: control blood sugar and lipids, adhere to CPAP if you use it (treating sleep apnea lowers cardiovascular risk), sodium reduction, weight management, regular aerobic activity.  
- Review levothyroxine dosing and TSH — both under‑ and over‑replacement affect bone and cardiovascular risk. Review gabapentin and any meds that interact with cognition/fall risk.

Red flags (tell your PCP or go to ER immediately)
- New focal weakness, difficulty speaking, sudden vision changes, severe headache, chest pain, shortness of breath, or a current very high BP reading with symptoms.

Offer to help further
- I can draft a concise message or checklist you can bring to Dr. Wilson or help word a referral request to Colorado Cardiology.  
- If you want, confirm your full current med list and whether the 320 reading was systolic and recent; I’ll tailor the questions and a one‑page handout for your visit.

